\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{We identify a module with its fully-qualified name, writing ``module $q$'' for the module named $q$.}}{3}{section*.8}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{A module body is a sequence of imports $\vec {\iota }$ followed by a sequence of statements $\vec {s}$; since imports are not statements, they can appear neither in blocks (and hence not in function bodies) nor after the first non-import statement.}}{3}{section*.9}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{the statements of}}{3}{section*.10}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{(metafunction $\mathrm {body}$, Figure\nobreakspace {}\ref {fig:eval-expr})}}{3}{section*.11}%
\contentsline {deleted}{Deleted: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{\ rather than one written in the source}}{3}{section*.12}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The metafunction $\mathrm {assigns}$ extends by union from individual statements to blocks, and $\mathrm {imports}$ from individual imports to import sequences; on a module body $\vec {\iota }\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\vec {s}$, they act on $\vec {s}$ and $\vec {\iota }$ respectively.\ }}{6}{section*.14}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The \emph {dependencies} $\mathrm {deps}(q)$ of a module $q$ (Figure\nobreakspace {}\ref {fig:modules}) are the modules its import prefix imports, together with their parents. Since imports precede statements, when a PurePy\xspace program runs under Python, every module in $\mathrm {deps}(q)$ is loaded before any statement of $q$ runs.}}{6}{section*.16}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Suppose $\mathcal {M}(q) = \vec {\iota }\tmspace +\thickmuskip {.2777em}\vec {s}$ with $\vdash \vec {\iota } : \Gamma $ and $\Gamma \vdash \mathrm {body}(q) : \Delta $.\ }}{13}{section*.24}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{\emph {members} of $q$ as $\mathrm {members}(q)$\ }}{13}{section*.25}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The members $\mathrm {members}(q)$ also contain\ }}{13}{section*.26}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{; the two parts are disjoint, because the \textcolor {gray}{\textnormal {\textsf {module}}} rule prohibits a module from binding a name that is also the name of one of its submodules (\S \ref {sec:submodule-clash})}}{13}{section*.27}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Rule \textcolor {gray}{\textnormal {\textsf {qualified-submodule}}} requires the submodule to be among the dependencies of the referring module $q_0$. This ensures it is loaded wherever the reference can be evaluated; Python raises \mbox {\lstinline [style=base16python]{AttributeError}} on the attribute path of an unloaded submodule.}}{13}{section*.28}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Local or interleaved imports: an \mbox {\lstinline [style=base16python]{import}} or \mbox {\lstinline [style=base16python]{from}} statement inside a function or other block, or after a non-import statement. PurePy\xspace permits imports only at the start of a module; Python allows them anywhere.\ }}{18}{section*.31}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{A module binding a name that is also the name of one of its submodules (\S \ref {sec:submodule-clash}).}}{18}{section*.32}%
\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Referring to a submodule the referring module does not itself import: \mbox {\lstinline [style=base16python]{import a}} followed by \mbox {\lstinline [style=base16python]{a.b.f()}} is rejected unless \mbox {\lstinline [style=base16python]{a.b}} is among the module's own dependencies. Python permits the reference whenever some earlier import, anywhere in the program, loaded \mbox {\lstinline [style=base16python]{a.b}}.}}{18}{section*.33}%
\contentsline {replaced}{Replaced: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Module and program evaluation are given in Figure\nobreakspace {}\ref {fig:eval-modules}; importing names from a loaded module in Figure\nobreakspace {}\ref {fig:eval-imports}. The loading judgement $q \Rightarrow _{\mathsf {load}} \rho $ evaluates the import prefix of $q$ and then its statements, mirroring the well-formedness judgement $\vdash _{\mathcal {M}} q$; the mutual recursion between loading and evaluation is grounded by the acyclicity of $G_{\mathcal {M}}$. Loading is deterministic; write $\rho _q$ for the unique $\rho $ such that $q \Rightarrow _{\mathsf {load}} \rho $. Since imports precede statements, every module a statement can reference is loaded before the statement runs, and since loading has no observable effects, reloading a module is unobservable; the semantics therefore needs no cache of loaded modules.\ }}{19}{section*.36}%
